ar to an animal that is unable to  control its urges throughout the years  sobic has been referred to by many names  including Sir Beck so ket Sukkos in  ancient Greece and of course sobic as  we've come to know him by today he was  first mentioned in the Pyramid Texts of  the old kingdom which were widely  regarded as some of the oldest sects to  exist when sobic first made an  appearance it was in the ancient  egyptian city of shed yet the ancient  Greeks referred to this  city as Crocodylus essentially meaning  city of the crocodile or crocodile city  the city of shed yet was part of a  larger region known as phi m and that is  why sobic was given the nickname lord of  phi m as one would expect in the city of  the crocodile so Baek had quite a cult  following there was a large temple built  in his name to honor the God the temple  was home to a crocodile but people  called the son of sobic and it was  worshipped as the manifestation of sobic  it was even adorned with gifts of gold  and gems the crocodile was fed better  than most people within the city with a  diet consisting of luxuries such as meat  wine and milk with honey when the  crocodile died much like the Pharaohs of  Egypt it was mummified and then replaced  by another crocodile who assumed the  role of son of sobic when observing what  took place in the city itself there were  numerous Greek historians who recorded  that anyone who was killed by a  crocodile considered to be the son of  sobic was considered to be divine the  victims would be embalmed and buried in  a sacred coffin and special funeral  rites will be performed by the priests  of the Nile it may seem strange to us  now but some of the ancient Egyptians  kept crocodiles just like house pets  believing that feed in a crocodile would  mean they would receive so bex blessing  despite sobic being worshipped in the  old kingdom it wasn't until the Middle  Kingdom when the influence of sobic  really began to grow during this period  he was often associated with the god  Horus and there are even numerous  depictions of the two gods fused  together the head of a falcon placed on  the body of a crocodile it's also  believed that Horus may have taken the  form of a crocodile when attempting to  retrieve the body parts of Osiris that  set had scattered around the rivers of  Egypt there is a story where the four  sons of Horus were drowning in the River  Nile but sobic appeared and gathered  them all up in a giant net placing the  children back on land now this story  raised a very interesting theory to me  who sobic been considered so  unpredictable and temperamental you  would assume that he saved the children  of Horus because Horus was a god and in  turn saw his children but there is  another story that somewhat changed my  view on the situation when Seth killed  his brother Osiris he disembodied him  and through his body parts in the rivers  of Egypt one morning sobic saw the parts  of Osiris in the Nile and they looked so  appetizing to sobic that he could not  resist and began to eat the pots of us  are  as they floated by sobic was eventually  punished for defiling the God in this  manner by having his tongue removed  which is why when we see crocodiles in  Egyptian mythology they are rarely ever  depicted as having tongues if we compare  the two stories in one so Bakr pays to  show compassion to his fellow gods in  the other he completely disregards them  the way I interpret a difference between  these two stories is that sobic is  constantly described as being very  instinctual and almost primitive and  he's approached to his duties when he  saw the children of horus drowning in  the River Nile I don't think the thought  process involved saving the children of  a god I think so Beck's actions were  purely instinctual in the same way we  see animals to protect the offspring of  other animals or even when pets like  cats and dogs are overprotective of  their owners children to me this  explains why when 